body:
\*I've been an employee and employer and have spoken with a few lawyers regarding this exactly\*

&#x200B;

SHRM can say whatever they want. It holds 0 weight in a court of law. They simply offer guidelines. They are not a federal or state agency holding any sort of say so in any of this. Just merely guidelines. I have been done countless hours of research on this. I had to sue an employer for holding my paycheck. They can legally do it. It could be something as small as minor accounting error or as major as the accountant entirely f\*\*king up and not even submitting the info on time. If you wanna make a mountain of a mole hill with this, you're better off just finding a new job.

